# Fixture for `just diagnostic-stream-smoke`: the POSITIVE CONTROL.
#
# Must compile cleanly — exit 0, LLVM IR on stdout, nothing on stderr.
#
# This fixture is what makes the gate meaningful. The other two assert that bad
# input is rejected; without a good input that is accepted, every assertion would
# also hold for a compiler that rejected literally everything. A gate that cannot
# tell "correctly rejected" from "completely broken" is not a gate.
#
# `int_to_string` and `print` are used ON PURPOSE despite needing no import: they
# resolve importlessly by design (ir_header emits `declare i64 @int_to_string(i64)`
# unconditionally, and `print` is a compiler intrinsic), which keeps this fixture
# independent of the prelude and stdlib.
fn main() -> Unit !{IO} =
  print(int_to_string(5))
